  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{6}=1?$",
  "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-2$ and $k=4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-2,4).$",
